better* iwctl
=============

After wracking my brain over how I'm going to do this, I'm just going to
literally wing it.

This is the first piece of the new iwctl. It connects to iwd over a local
Unix socket, sends ping!, and expects pong! back. On success it prints:

    ping! pong!

Start the daemon in one terminal (as root):

    ./src/iwd

Then run the client in another (also as root):

    ./ctl/iwctl

The default socket is /run/eiwd.sock, set by CONTROLSOCKET in config.mk.
IWD_CONTROL_SOCKET overrides the path at runtime; set the same value for
both processes. The socket is created with mode 0600, so the client needs
to run as its owner, normally root (sorry). The exchange uses SOCK_SEQPACKET.
It allows 16 active clients since idk, one of these days someone will need
that I suppose.

Source map, relative to the project root for any contributors:

    ctl/main.c          client connection, exchange, and result
    shared/control.h    protocol definitions (very large tmb)
    src/control.c       listener and client handling in ELL's event loop
    src/control.h       control_init() and control_exit()
    src/main.c          starts and stops the listener with iwd

Normal shutdown removes the socket. Existing paths are never overwritten;
a stale socket left by a crash needs manual removal after confirming that
the old daemon is no longer running. TL;DR just your regular dameon/client
socket stuffs.

*footnote: ahem, the author (me) makes no guarantees that this is going to
be good.

